X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Press Democrat": "https://cei.sonoma.edu/Blessed-acorn"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Press Democrat": "https://cei.sonoma.edu/Blessed-acorn"}
X-SOURCE-ID:pressdemocrat
SUMMARY:Blessing of the Acorn
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261012T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261012T130000
UID:260910aRDdPgCLFka07GNwman7Cg
DESCRIPTION:In collaboration with Anderson Valley Land Trust\, join us to 
 harvest tanoak acorns\, learn about their value\, traditions around them\,
  and how to select the best acorns\, care for them\, and prepare them for 
 eating. Acorns are a traditional food source for indigenous people through
 out the United States. Historically\, they have provided up to 40 percent 
 of sustenance for Pomo people\, and serve as a rich source of protein and 
 fats. In the North Bay\, the most highly prized acorn comes from the tanoa
 k tree\, or chishkale\, the Pomo word for tanoak. This member of the beech
  family is not a true oak\, but a cousin whose acorn comes adorned with a 
 furry showy hat said to be the envy of the other oaks with their flat or v
 aguely bumpy caps.
LOCATION:Galbreath Wildlands Preserve
URL:https://cei.sonoma.edu/Blessed-acorn
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
