X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar Livewhale": "https://calendar.santarosa.edu/event/24107-marking-the-flesh-revealing-the-tattoo-practices"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_livewhale
SUMMARY:Marking the Flesh: Revealing the Tattoo Practices of the Nile Vall
 ey
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261018T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261018T171500
DTSTAMP:20261008T145253Z
UID:20261018T230000Z-24107@calendar.santarosa.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261018T160000
ATTACH:https://calendar.santarosa.edu/live/image/gid/3/width/80/height/80/
 crop/1/src_region/0,0,500,500/745_cs_-_making_the_flesh_lecture.PNG
CATEGORIES:Department Events,District Events
DESCRIPTION:The North Coast Chapter of the Archaeological Institute of Ame
 rica invites you to the following lecture:  Marking the Flesh: Revealing t
 he Tattoo Practices of the Nile ValleyAbstract: For more than a century\, 
 archaeologists knew that some ancient Egyptians and Nubians had tattoos—
 but only a few tattooed people had been identified across Egypt and Nubia'
 s vast ancient history.  Over the past decade\, however\, new technology a
 nd growing interest have dramatically changed our evidence for and underst
 anding of tattooing. This lecture will share the stories of these tattooed
  people and the remarkable legacy of tattoo practices they represent—a t
 radition that began over 5\,000 years ago and continues today.*Dr. Anne Au
 stin is an Associate Professor of History at the University of Missouri St
 . Louis (UMSL). Her research combines the fields of osteology and Egyptolo
 gy in order to document medicine and disease in the past.  Event Details: 
  • Sunday\, October 18\, 2026\, SRJC Petaluma Student Center (https://ma
 ps.santarosa.edu/student-center)\, 4:00-5:15 p.m.  • This lecture is fre
 e and open to the public  • *This presentation may contain images of hum
 an remains.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261008T215253Z
LOCATION:Student Center - Petaluma Campus
URL:https://calendar.santarosa.edu/event/24107-marking-the-flesh-revealing
 -the-tattoo-practices
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The North Coast Chapter of the Archaeolo
 gical Institute of America invites you to the following lecture:</p><p>  M
 arking the Flesh: Revealing the Tattoo Practices of the Nile Valley</p><p>
 <strong>Abstract:</strong> For more than a century\, archaeologists knew t
 hat some ancient Egyptians and Nubians had tattoos—but only a few tattoo
 ed people had been identified across Egypt and Nubia's vast ancient histor
 y.</p><p>  Over the past decade\, however\, new technology and growing int
 erest have dramatically changed our evidence for and understanding of tatt
 ooing. This lecture will share the stories of these tattooed people and th
 e remarkable legacy of tattoo practices they represent—a tradition that 
 began over 5\,000 years ago and continues today.*</p><p><strong>Dr. Anne A
 ustin</strong> is an Associate Professor of History at the University of M
 issouri St. Louis (UMSL). Her research combines the fields of osteology an
 d Egyptology in order to document medicine and disease in the past.</p><p>
   Event Details:<br />  • Sunday\, October 18\, 2026\, <a href="https://
 maps.santarosa.edu/student-center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRJC Pet
 aluma Student Center</a>\, 4:00-5:15 p.m.<br />  • This lecture is free 
 and open to the public<br />  • *This presentation may contain images of
  human remains.</p>
