 a toward dignity\, agency\, and self-representation. The women within thes
 e pages are dancers\, artists\, bead workers\, teachers\, mothers\, daught
 ers\, grandmothers\, language keepers\, and land and water defenders. They
  are culture bearers\, but they are not defined by what they carry for oth
 ers." — Julian Cook\, Co-Creator</p><p>  A Portrait Series by <a href="h
 ttps://www.lexmexart.com/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexa LexMe
 x Treviño</a> in collaboration with the SRJC Multicultural Museum.</p><p>
 <em>This exhibit is funded by a grant from the Santa Rosa Junior College F
 oundation Randolph Newman Cultural Enrichment Endowment.</em></p><p><a hre
 f="https://museum.santarosa.edu/event/standing-her-power-portraits-beyond-
 colonial-frame" target="_blank" rel="noopener">For more information\, visi
 t the SRJC Multicultural Museum website.</a></p>
